# BOHEMIA SIDE QUEST — PLAYABLE .bq (canon machine layer)
# S11 — SIXTY SECONDS OF RAIN
# Shape: the clock quest. No villain, no negotiation, just minutes. Faction wires:
# BLUES, HOMELESS. Grounded hard in the real: the Las Vegas wash floods from rain
# you never see fall, twenty miles upstream, in dry sunshine, and the flood channels
# and storm tunnels people actually live in go from dust to chest-high in minutes.
# The whole quest is that one true fact turned into a fork. Systems AS the mechanic:
# time is the only currency here and every option spends it somewhere different.
# The lesson underneath, never spoken: the seconds you spend making sure are the
# seconds somebody else drowns in. No proper names. CLOUT rides the deed: going
# down the tunnel yourself is #quiet (nobody upstairs ever knows), ringing the
# market bell is #notable, breaking the grate open in front of everyone is
# #reckless. Waiting for proof is the failure, and the failure is #quiet too,
# because nobody sees you not act.

# ---- QUESTBOOK STUDY CITATIONS (QUEST STUDY LAW, 7/26/26) -------------------
# The corpus this quest is actually built from. Every id below resolves in
# records/BOHEMIA_QUESTBOOK_LAW_INDEX.json (3,672 citable findings mined out of
# the 152 studied quests in questbook/), and gates/quest_study_gate.js verifies
# the id AND the quoted law title verbatim. A citation is a claim the machine
# can check, never a name-drop.
# @STUDY Q016.W9  THE SOFT-TIMER ENVIRONMENT
#   applied: no on-screen timer: the pressure is the sky and a woman with bad knees, and asking her to be certain is how you spend the clock.
# @STUDY Q004.P2  W2 (obvious-good trap
#   applied: the reflexive good deed (ring the bell, wake everyone) genuinely saves them and genuinely brings the people who check what got left behind.
# @STUDY Q030.W9  ATTRITION CASCADES
#   applied: the failure is not dramatic; it is the small delay that snowballs into the wash filling while you were being reasonable.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@QUEST   bq_sixty_seconds   Sixty Seconds Of Rain
@ACT     1
@FACTION BLUES
@ONCE    true

@ROLE watcher  REQ  faction=BLUES     reads_the_sky=true
@ROLE tunnel   REQ  faction=HOMELESS  sleeps_in_the_wash=true

@STAGE 10
  @LOG Storm upstream, twenty miles out. Not a drop falling here. The wash is bone dry and the sky over it's blue.
  @DO show_objective 10
@STAGE 20
  @LOG People are sleeping in the culvert. The water gets there before the sound does.
  @DO show_objective 20
  @DO complete_objective 10
@STAGE 30 COMPLETE #quiet
  @LOG Went down myself and walked them out with a hand on each shoulder. It came through eight minutes later. Nobody up top ever knew.
  @DO bond tunnel +25
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 31 COMPLETE #notable
  @LOG Rang the market bell. Half the block came to help and a few came to see what got left unattended. Everyone in the culvert got out.
  @DO faction BLUES +8
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 32 COMPLETE #reckless
  @LOG Broke the grate off the culvert mouth with a crowd watching, screaming into a hole in the ground. They got out. So did everything that lived down there.
  @DO faction BLUES +4
  @DO set_flag opened_the_deep
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 33 FAIL #quiet
  @LOG Waited to be sure. It was sunny the whole time, right up until the wash filled. Nobody watched me decide nothing.
  @DO bond tunnel -20

@OBJ 10  "Believe the sky you cannot see"   target=watcher
@OBJ 20  "Get them out of the wash"         target=tunnel

# =====================================================================
# THE ASK. The theme is the first line. She is not asking for a favor, she
# is handing you a countdown and she cannot run it herself.
# =====================================================================
@TALK sky  speaker=watcher  entry=stage>=10,stage<20
  @SAY Look up. Blue, all the way across, and that's exactly the problem. #dread
  @SAY It's raining hard twenty miles north where nobody lives. All of it comes here. The wash doesn't care that the sun is out over your head.
  @SAY I have maybe twenty minutes of usefulness left and two bad knees. There are people sleeping in the culvert. Go.
  @OPT "How sure are you?"                 [gate: none]  -> sure
  @OPT "Going now."                        [gate: none]  -> down       @DO learn water_is_coming
  @OPT "You've been wrong before."       [gate: none]  TRAP -> wrong
  @OPT (already moving)                    [gate: none]  SILENCE -> down  @DO learn water_is_coming
  @NOVERB "Ask her to come with me"
@END

@TALK sure  speaker=watcher
  @SAY Sure enough that I'm standing in the street telling a stranger instead of sitting down. #dread
  @SAY Nobody ever gets a wall of water on the horizon. You get a sound, and by the time you get the sound you're already in it.
  @OPT "Then I'm gone."                   [gate: none]  -> down  @DO learn water_is_coming
  @OPT (turn and run)                      [gate: none]  SILENCE -> down  @DO learn water_is_coming
@END

# THE TRAP: making her prove it is the only genuinely fatal option in the quest.
# It costs the exact thing that matters, and the game never says so out loud.
@TALK wrong  speaker=watcher
  @SAY Twice. Both times I was wrong I was early, and both times everybody got wet and annoyed and alive. #dread
  @SAY You want me to be certain. Certainty out here arrives about four minutes after it's useful. Stand there and wait for it if you like.
  @OPT "Then I wait for the rain."         [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 33
  @OPT "No. Moving."                       [gate: none]  -> down  @DO learn water_is_coming
@END

@TALK down  speaker=watcher
  @SAY Culvert mouth, past the dead lot. There will be four or five of them and at least one who won't believe you. #dread
  @DO set_stage 20
@END

# =====================================================================
# THE MOUTH. He is not stupid and he is not stubborn. He has been moved
# along by people yelling at him his entire life and this sounds like that.
# =====================================================================
@TALK mouth  speaker=tunnel  entry=stage>=20
  @SAY Sun is out. Whatever this is, say it fast, because it usually ends with me carrying my bedroll somewhere worse. #wary
  @SAY I've heard flood, fire, raid, sweep and inspection. Four of those were somebody wanting this spot.
  @OPT "It's raining north. Come up with me now."   [gate: none]  -> hand
  @OPT "Ringing the market bell. Everyone comes."     [gate: none]  -> bell
  @OPT "Then I'm taking the grate off."              [gate: none]  -> grate
  @NOVERB "Tell him the water is already here"
@END

@TALK hand  speaker=tunnel
  @SAY You came down here. Nobody comes down here. #wary
  @SAY All right. All right, I'm up. There are two more asleep at the bend and one of them will swing at you before he's awake. Take my hand, don't take his.
  @OPT "One at a time. Go."           [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 30
  @OPT (haul him up and go back for the others)  [gate: none]  SILENCE -> END  @DO set_stage 30
@END

@TALK bell  speaker=tunnel
  @SAY The bell brings help and it brings shoppers. Half of them will be pulling us out and half will be checking what we left behind. #wary
  @SAY Ring it anyway. I'd rather be robbed than drowned, and that's a sentence I didn't think I'd say today.
  @OPT "Ringing it."                  [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 31
  @OPT "No. Quietly. Just you and me and the bend."  [gate: none]  -> hand
@END

@TALK grate  speaker=tunnel
  @SAY You're going to open the whole mouth. In daylight. With people watching where we sleep. #dread
  @SAY Everyone gets out. And everyone learns exactly where the deep goes and who's in it. You can't put a hole back.
  @OPT "Open it. Everyone out."       [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 32
  @OPT (say nothing and pull the grate anyway)  [gate: none]  SILENCE -> END  @DO set_stage 32
  @OPT "Fine. Quietly. Your hand."    [gate: none]  -> hand
@END
